## Post-release checks

<!-- TBD: other checks -->

- [ ] Everything on stdout is JSON lines (look at `docker logs`; no bare text)
- [ ] Requesting any endpoint adds an `http request` line
- [ ] The `version` on the `service start` line is this release's short commit hash (not `unknown`; the CI build passed `--build-arg APP_VERSION=<short commit hash>`)
- [ ] The `GET /health` body has all four fields (`ok`, `version`, `db`, `uptime_s`), and with the database stopped it still returns 200 with `ok=false` in the body, within 2 seconds; where `GET /health/ready` exists, it returns 200 and returns 503 when a dependency is cut off, and the deploy config probes it before switching traffic
- [ ] At least one scheduled / background job has finished a run, with the matching `job end` in the logs (for high-frequency polling, a `job heartbeat`)
- [ ] If the service starts child processes, headless browsers, or long-lived workers: the container has init, memory and process-count limits, and a restart policy (compose `init: true`, `mem_limit`, `pids_limit`, `restart:`; `docker run` `--init`, `--memory`, `--pids-limit`, `--restart`), and the scheduled cleanup is running (the logs show the cleanup job's `job end` or `job heartbeat`)
- [ ] Every API key the project uses is a CI variable (written from the password manager by the sync command, not in an env file); each key has a row in the ops registry whose note is the password-manager item name

The full log contract is in the "Log contract" section of `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`.
